&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fttj3um1nkm4y713kwrk3.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fttj3um1nkm4y713kwrk3.png" alt="ArtCraft Overview" width="800" height="451"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A few weeks ago, a piece of news hit the tech world that astonished everyone. A developer revealed that they had replicated 7 Adobe applications using Opus 5.5—without writing a single line of code. These apps largely mimicked Adobe's UI and features.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When I first read this news, I was quite surprised because replicating a software so closely using AI felt surreal to me.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In this article, we will test &lt;strong&gt;ArtCraft&lt;/strong&gt;, an open-source project that mimics applications from &lt;strong&gt;Adobe&lt;/strong&gt; as well as &lt;strong&gt;Microsoft Office&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Cancellation Fees
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As you know, to download and use Adobe apps, you pay a monthly or yearly subscription fee. However, Adobe doesn't want you to cancel your subscription once you've signed up, and they hit you with a cancellation fee if you try.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/possibilistic/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Brandon Thomas&lt;/a&gt;, the creator of ArtCraft, noted that Adobe's cancellation fees were the exact reason he started building this project.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Thomas explained that he created ArtCraft using &lt;strong&gt;Anthropic Claude Opus 5.5&lt;/strong&gt;, utilizing AI assistance for reverse engineering without writing any manual code. ArtCraft is written in the Rust programming language, and a WebAssembly version is also available for web usage. However, I should note that I couldn't locate the web version during my research.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;em&amp;gt;"We are going to replace 'Google Search', manufacture our own smartphones, and build a better infrastructure. We will get rid of all the ads and put an end to that terrible mess that has overtaken the entire tech world."

&amp;lt;strong&amp;gt;- Thomas&amp;lt;/strong&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/em&amp;gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


  The ArtCraft Ecosystem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Artcraft currently provides open-source alternatives for 7 Adobe apps, several MS Office applications, and other tools:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;PhotoCraft&lt;/strong&gt; - Adobe Photoshop&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;VectorCraft&lt;/strong&gt; - Adobe Illustrator&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;FilmCraft&lt;/strong&gt; - Adobe Premiere Pro&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;LightCraft&lt;/strong&gt; - Adobe Lightroom&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;EffectCraft&lt;/strong&gt; - Adobe After Effects&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;DesignCraft&lt;/strong&gt; - Adobe InDesign&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;PdfCraft&lt;/strong&gt; - Adobe Acrobat&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;FigCraft&lt;/strong&gt; - Figma&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;WordCraft&lt;/strong&gt; - Microsoft Word&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;GridCraft&lt;/strong&gt; - Microsoft Excel&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;DeckCraft&lt;/strong&gt; - Microsoft PowerPoint&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;SoundCraft&lt;/strong&gt; - Adobe Audition&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;CADCraft&lt;/strong&gt; - Autodesk AutoCAD&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fvwejr8f5m7sco7wc2tao.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fvwejr8f5m7sco7wc2tao.png" alt="ArtCraft Tools 1" width="800" height="297"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fwq27tte7npskthp3ewwr.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fwq27tte7npskthp3ewwr.png" alt="ArtCraft Tools 2" width="799" height="298"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These look very similar to the original apps, and many functions are genuinely identical. I won't be testing every single program in this article; instead, I'll select the most widely used ones and try to compare their similarities.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  PhotoCraft
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let's first take a look at the general UI. Looking at the overall user interface, we can see it bears a strong resemblance to Adobe Photoshop.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F8y2dzpv4mgmhjoebt9ev.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F8y2dzpv4mgmhjoebt9ev.png" alt="PhotoCraft UI" width="800" height="426"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Since I am not a professional graphic designer, I couldn't test the advanced features, but as a normal user, all the functions I needed worked smoothly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When checking the available export formats, we see the &lt;strong&gt;.psd&lt;/strong&gt; format, which allows us to open our designs directly in Adobe Photoshop.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fr3j1tzejs2cs7dmzuzl0.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fr3j1tzejs2cs7dmzuzl0.png" alt="PhotoCraft Export Formats" width="630" height="294"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As for performance, it uses about 150MB of RAM while idle, and around 300MB when working on an image. The file size of PhotoCraft is around 67 MB.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fg2o5hbaj57qoes31sydb.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fg2o5hbaj57qoes31sydb.png" alt="PhotoCraft Performance" width="644" height="56"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  FilmCraft
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Adobe alternative that surprised me the most was FilmCraft. I've previously tried Premiere Pro alternatives like Davinci Resolve and CapCut. However, the UIs of those programs differed from Premiere Pro, which is one of the barriers preventing professional Premiere Pro editors from transitioning. With FilmCraft, I think transitioning from Premiere will be much easier for many people.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fl2hwpfkfge6nssgje84y.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fl2hwpfkfge6nssgje84y.png" alt="FilmCraft UI" width="800" height="425"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fp26j4ul7va2jux68z2hl.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fp26j4ul7va2jux68z2hl.png" alt="FilmCraft Panels" width="609" height="734"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are plenty of parameters you can choose from in the export options. I'd even like to share a sample video I edited using FilmCraft:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  WordCraft
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This program is an alternative to Microsoft Word. Although I've tried Word alternatives like LibreOffice Writer and Google Docs in the past, WordCraft was the one I picked up and learned to use the fastest.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fl6mutxh85y2fig5ok7yx.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fl6mutxh85y2fig5ok7yx.png" alt="WordCraft UI" width="800" height="424"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  FigCraft
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Figma is one of the programs that imposes the most restrictions on free accounts. The free version has critical limitations, such as project creation limits. FigCraft gives us infinite possibilities alongside its similarity to Figma.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fpzwcdkakx9kj5v0wfktm.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fpzwcdkakx9kj5v0wfktm.png" alt="FigCraft UI" width="799" height="423"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After designing a sample demo, I tried exporting it as a &lt;strong&gt;.fig&lt;/strong&gt; file and opening it in Figma. And indeed, all elements opened seamlessly inside Figma.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fryzeiyw1nfywqt8ae363.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fryzeiyw1nfywqt8ae363.png" alt="FigCraft Export" width="522" height="261"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fxinovmk2x0z1iaqkhpjf.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fxinovmk2x0z1iaqkhpjf.png" alt="FigCraft in Figma" width="799" height="423"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Feedback and Project Goals
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No matter how much headlines in the design world claim "ArtCraft will replace Adobe," the reality is a bit different. Yes, Vibe Coding has replicated all these apps with high precision. But that doesn't mean the apps are 100% identical yet. In some tests conducted, PhotoCraft passed very few out of 30 effect tests. Likewise, users noticed many flaws during professional use. For example, here is a piece of &lt;a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49960138" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;feedback&lt;/a&gt; left for PhotoCraft on October 12, 2026:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;“1. Tried to select text; it feels like the selection tool doesn't align with the cursor, it stays off to the side.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;2. Tried to edit text; moving the size slider takes about 3 seconds, but nothing happens (the text size doesn't change).&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;3. Everything feels sluggish: making selections, transitioning, etc.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;4. Cannot expand or collapse folders.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;5. Keyboard shortcuts are displayed everywhere, but pressing those shortcuts doesn't trigger any action.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;6. Tried to move an object elsewhere, the app froze for a moment.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;7. Clicked somewhere, and the app automatically selected one of the side panels and hid the other. Now I can't resize, collapse, or reset the panels back..."&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Of course, many of these bugs no longer exist. The project is actively developed day by day, and bugs are being fixed. But keep in mind that the project is not a finished product yet and is continuously evolving. While these apps are sufficient for simple daily tasks, you need to evaluate the risks for professional use. Ultimately, the project is open-source, completely free, and an unfinished product open to updates.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Although the project's creators initially claimed it would be 100% identical to Adobe apps within a month, they later walked back that statement, saying, "reaching 99% will take months, if not years." Whether the project will face legal troubles remains a mystery.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Vibe Coding
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The primary reason this project was able to come to life is the concept of &lt;strong&gt;Vibe Coding&lt;/strong&gt;. Unlike traditional programming, in Vibe Coding, a developer doesn't write code line by line. Instead, we explain the idea and application functionality to LLMs, and the LLMs execute those tasks on our behalf.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The reality brought to light by ArtCraft reveals two major truths:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Previously, building prototypes for 7 different Adobe apps required years and a team of dozens of engineers. Now, via Vibe Coding, a single programmer can write this entire ecosystem from scratch in just a few weeks. This is a first sign of how small teams and freelancers will challenge giant corporations in the future.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Vibe Coding can build 80% of a visible app—its user interface (UI) and core functions—in seconds. However, the remaining 20%, such as performance optimization and edge cases, still require deep engineering knowledge.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Final Word
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ArtCraft isn't fully ready to wipe apps like Photoshop, Illustrator, Premiere Pro, etc., which have spent years specializing in this sector, completely off our workflow today. Potential legal risks are just one of the major hurdles facing the project.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, this project shows us a very crucial truth. A single individual can now shake the throne of a monopoly. It shows that the future of programming is changing completely and that we are stepping into a freer, open-source technology era.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Thank you for your attention.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h4&gt;Join us so you don't miss new posts&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://samirrhashimov.substack.com/subscribe?" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Subscribe now&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When an AI Integration Guide and Its Recommended SDK Installation Don't Align&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An integration guide can provide a seemingly complete implementation path while leaving an important compatibility question unresolved.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I recently examined a guide published by an AI inference infrastructure provider. It demonstrates how developers can build a streaming AI chat application using a popular React-based AI SDK.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The guide covers package installation, provider configuration, server-side streaming and frontend chat functionality.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, the relationship between its recommended package installation and frontend implementation raises an interesting question.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Are they targeting the same SDK interface?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The recommended installation&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The guide recommends installing:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;npm install ai @ai-sdk/openai&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It then demonstrates configuring an OpenAI-compatible provider.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The installation instructions don't specify package versions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That detail becomes important when the guide introduces its frontend implementation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The frontend implementation&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The example imports its React chat hook using:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;import { useChat } from "ai/react";&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It subsequently uses an interface containing:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;const {&lt;br&gt;
  messages,&lt;br&gt;
  input,&lt;br&gt;
  handleInputChange,&lt;br&gt;
  handleSubmit,&lt;br&gt;
  isLoading&lt;br&gt;
} = useChat({&lt;br&gt;
  api: "/api/chat",&lt;br&gt;
});&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Message rendering also accesses:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;message.content&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These are characteristics of an older SDK interface.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;What the current SDK documentation demonstrates&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Current documentation for the SDK uses a different React package entry point:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;import { useChat } from "@ai-sdk/react";&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The current implementation also demonstrates a transport-based integration pattern.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;const { messages, sendMessage } = useChat();&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Message representation and rendering have also evolved.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This means the differences extend beyond the import statement.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The hook interface and message-handling approach are different.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The assumption created by the integration guide&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Consider the developer following the instructions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;They:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Install the recommended packages.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Configure the inference provider.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Implement the server-side streaming endpoint.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Follow the supplied React example.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Attempt to complete the chat interface.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At this point, the developer encounters an important compatibility question.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The installation instructions don't identify an older package version corresponding to the frontend implementation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A developer could reasonably assume that the recommended installation supports the interface demonstrated in the same guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, the published instructions don't establish that compatibility.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The developer may need to investigate package versions, reconcile the interface differences or adapt the frontend implementation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The responsibility boundary&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are two distinct responsibilities here.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Provider integration: Connecting the application to the inference infrastructure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Frontend SDK integration: Ensuring that the installed dependencies support the React interface used by the application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The infrastructure provider can abstract the inference connection without automatically resolving every compatibility requirement in the surrounding application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The developer remains responsible for reconciling those components.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Potential implementation consequences&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When the compatibility relationship isn't established by the guide, developers may need to perform additional work:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Investigate compatible dependency versions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Identify the appropriate frontend interface.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Adapt the supplied implementation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Resolve dependency differences before completing the integration.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This introduces additional implementation work along a path intended to simplify adoption.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It doesn't establish that the inference API itself is malfunctioning.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The unresolved question concerns the compatibility of the documented installation and frontend implementation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Evidence limitation&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This investigation compares a published AI infrastructure integration guide with current documentation for the underlying React-based AI SDK.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No live package installation, compilation or runtime test was performed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Consequently, this finding concerns the documented interface discrepancy and the compatibility responsibility left unresolved.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It should not be interpreted as an experimentally confirmed application failure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Conclusion&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Integration guides communicate more than installation instructions and code examples.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;They also establish expectations about compatibility, supported interfaces and the responsibilities developers must handle themselves.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When those expectations aren't sufficiently established, developers may have to perform additional investigation before completing the intended implementation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is an important dimension of developer implementation experiences.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;Independent implementation investigation by DaxMac.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've spent some time recently improving Galaxy Movies. It's a movie and TV discovery app I've been building with Next.js.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A lot of the recent work has focused on making the experience smoother and cleaning up parts of the codebase.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Improving the watchlist
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The watchlist now gives new users a better starting point. When it's empty, there's a trending section with titles they can add with one click.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The idea is to make it easier for someone to discover their first few titles instead of landing on an empty page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's a simplified look at the empty state logic.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;watchlist&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;WatchlistEmptyState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;TrendingRail&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Start Here"&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;onAdd&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;addToWatchlist&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;WatchlistEmptyState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;WatchlistGrid&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;titles&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;watchlist&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;I also added a small hint for visitors using iPhones who haven't installed the app yet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Smoother navigation
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I added a progress bar at the top of the page to make route changes feel less abrupt. Existing loading indicators are still used when content updates without a full page transition.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I also fixed an issue with provider filtered searches. Previously, titles could briefly appear before the streaming provider lookup finished. Now the loading state stays visible until the results are ready.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The important part is waiting for the provider lookup before displaying the filtered results.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;isLoadingProviders&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;SearchLoadingState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;MovieGrid&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;movies&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;filteredMovies&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


  Some cleanup behind the scenes
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've been consolidating repeated UI elements into shared components. This makes it easier to keep the same look and behavior across different parts of the app.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I also made changes to the sitemap. It now focuses more on pages that are useful for search engines instead of generating URLs that rarely bring in visitors.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Provider change pages now use their actual update dates for &lt;code&gt;lastmod&lt;/code&gt; rather than treating every page as if it changed whenever the sitemap was generated.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Keeping track of streaming changes
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Another change is a yearly streaming changes accumulator. It keeps track of titles arriving and leaving each provider throughout the year.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The stored data is organized by country, year and provider. That gives me a foundation for future yearly recaps without having to rebuild the history from scratch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A simplified example of the data structure looks like this.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;YearlyProviderChanges&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;arrived&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Record&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;departed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Record&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;YearlyChanges&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;country&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;year&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;providers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Record&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;YearlyProviderChanges&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


  What's next
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm continuing to work on Galaxy Movies and improve the experience as I go. There's still plenty I'd like to add, but I'm happy with how the project is coming together.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can check it out at &lt;a href="https://www.galaxymovies.app" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;galaxymovies.app&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The source code is available on &lt;a href="https://github.com/NickFasulo/galaxy-movies" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  TanStack Query in React
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;TanStack Query (formerly React Query) is a popular library used in React applications to manage server state. It simplifies fetching, caching, updating, and synchronizing data from APIs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Normally, we use &lt;code&gt;useState()&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;useEffect()&lt;/code&gt; to handle API requests. TanStack Query provides built-in features to manage these operations with less code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  1. Installation and Setup
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Install TanStack Query using npm:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npm &lt;span class="nb"&gt;install&lt;/span&gt; @tanstack/react-query
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Wrap your application with &lt;code&gt;QueryClientProvider&lt;/code&gt; in &lt;code&gt;main.jsx&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;QueryClient&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;QueryClientProvider&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@tanstack/react-query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;queryClient&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;QueryClient&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;createRoot&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getElementById&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;root&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;render&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;QueryClientProvider&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;queryClient&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;App&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;QueryClientProvider&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This makes TanStack Query available throughout the application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  2. useQuery()
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;useQuery()&lt;/code&gt; is used to fetch data from an API using GET requests.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useQuery&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@tanstack/react-query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;axios&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;axios&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;isPending&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;isError&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useQuery&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;queryKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;users&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="na"&gt;queryFn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;axios&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;https://dummyjson.com/users&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;users&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Important properties:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;queryKey&lt;/code&gt;: Uniquely identifies a query and its cached data.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;queryFn&lt;/code&gt;: Function responsible for fetching data.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;data&lt;/code&gt;: Contains the API response.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;isPending&lt;/code&gt;: Indicates that the query has no data yet and is pending.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;isError&lt;/code&gt;: Indicates that the query failed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  3. useMutation()
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;useMutation()&lt;/code&gt; is used to create, update, and delete data using POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE requests.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Example: Adding a new user.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useMutation&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@tanstack/react-query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;addUser&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useMutation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;mutationFn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;newUser&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;axios&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;https://dummyjson.com/users/add&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nx"&gt;newUser&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Execute mutation&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;addUser&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;mutate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;firstName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;John&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;age&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;25&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Important properties:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;mutationFn&lt;/code&gt;: Contains the API request.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;mutate()&lt;/code&gt;: Executes the mutation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;isPending&lt;/code&gt;: Indicates that the mutation is running.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;isSuccess&lt;/code&gt;: Indicates successful execution.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;isError&lt;/code&gt;: Indicates that the mutation failed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  4. useQueryClient()
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;useQueryClient()&lt;/code&gt; provides access to the Query Client. It helps manage cached API data.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One important use is refreshing cached data after a successful mutation.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useQueryClient&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useMutation&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@tanstack/react-query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;queryClient&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useQueryClient&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;addUser&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useMutation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;mutationFn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;addUserAPI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="na"&gt;onSuccess&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;queryClient&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;invalidateQueries&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;queryKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;users&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Here, &lt;code&gt;invalidateQueries()&lt;/code&gt; marks the existing users query as stale(or old). If the query is active, TanStack Query normally refetches it automatically.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;description: Step-by-step guide to integrate Google AI Studio Gemini 1.5 Flash in React with a secure serverless proxy to protect your API key.&lt;br&gt;
tags: react, ai, webdev, javascript&lt;br&gt;
canonical_url: &lt;a href="https://brainykits.in/google-ai-studio-website-templates" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://brainykits.in/google-ai-studio-website-templates&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Google AI Studio and the Gemini 1.5 API offer some of the fastest LLM inference speeds currently available on the market. With Gemini 1.5 Flash providing sub-second Time-To-First-Token (TTFT) and a generous 1-million-token context window, it has become the default choice for building AI wrappers, prompt widgets, and developer productivity tools.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, the #1 mistake front-end developers make when building with Google AI Studio is &lt;strong&gt;exposing their raw API key directly in client-side React code&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In this tutorial, we will build a production-ready React component connected to Google AI Studio via a secure serverless backend proxy.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  The Architecture: Why You Need a Backend Proxy
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Never put your &lt;code&gt;GEMINI_API_KEY&lt;/code&gt; inside &lt;code&gt;.env&lt;/code&gt; with a &lt;code&gt;VITE_&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;NEXT_PUBLIC_&lt;/code&gt; prefix. Client-side bundles are easily inspected in the browser DevTools Network tab, exposing your key to quota theft and unauthorized usage.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've been doing frontend for about twelve years, and a big part of that has been fighting bundle size. Code splitting. Lazy routes. Swapping heavy date libraries for lighter ones. Hunting down that one dependency that pulls in all of lodash. A Webpack 4 to 5 migration done with codemods because doing it by hand across a big codebase wasn't realistic.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But there was always one chunk of the bundle nobody touched: React and React DOM themselves. You just accepted that cost. It was the floor.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;TanStack Redact just lowered the floor. And the reason I think it matters isn't the clever engineering behind it (though there's plenty of that). It's how &lt;em&gt;useful&lt;/em&gt; it is on day one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What Redact actually is
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Redact is a smaller, React-compatible runtime for the browser. You add one plugin to your build config, and your existing React imports, components, hooks and third-party libraries run on Redact in the browser instead of React DOM.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's the whole pitch. No new API to learn. No &lt;code&gt;import { h } from 'somewhere-else'&lt;/code&gt;. No rewriting components. Your app code stays exactly the same.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It started as Tanner Linsley's experiment on his personal site and tanstack.com, where it's been running in production since April 2026. It stuck, other people started using it in production too, and on October 26 it shipped as &lt;strong&gt;0.2.0 with stable integrations for TanStack Start, Vite, Vinext and Next.js&lt;/strong&gt; (both App Router and Pages Router).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The usefulness is the headline
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most performance wins come with a migration cost. Move to a new framework, adopt a new rendering model, rewrite your state layer, retrain the team. The win is real, but you pay for it in weeks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Redact flips that. Here's a plain Vite + React app:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// vite.config.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;defineConfig&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;vite&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@vitejs/plugin-react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;redact&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@tanstack/redact/vite&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;defineConfig&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;plugins&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;redact&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()],&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;And TanStack Start:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// vite.config.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;defineConfig&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;vite&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;tanstackStart&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@tanstack/react-start/plugin/vite&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@vitejs/plugin-react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;redact&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@tanstack/redact/vite&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;defineConfig&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;ssr&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;noExternal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;plugins&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;tanstackStart&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;redact&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()],&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;For Next.js there's a &lt;code&gt;withRedact&lt;/code&gt; wrapper from &lt;code&gt;@tanstack/redact/next&lt;/code&gt; (the docs have the exact setup).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's it. Install, add a line, build, measure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Think about what this means in a real team:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No refactor PR.&lt;/strong&gt; Your diff is a config file and a &lt;code&gt;package.json&lt;/code&gt; line. Easy to review, easy to reason about.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Rollback is trivial.&lt;/strong&gt; Remove the plugin and you're back on React. That makes it a low-risk experiment instead of a bet-the-quarter decision.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Your ecosystem comes with you.&lt;/strong&gt; Component libraries, form libraries, query libraries — they keep importing &lt;code&gt;react&lt;/code&gt; and just get Redact in the browser.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Nobody has to learn anything.&lt;/strong&gt; New hires still write React. Your docs, your Storybook, your patterns all stay valid.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've led teams where the right performance move was blocked purely because it meant touching hundreds of components. A tool that gives you the win without that cost is the kind of thing that actually gets adopted.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The numbers
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The TanStack team built the same small app with React and with Redact in each framework, then added up the gzip size of all emitted client JS and CSS:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Framework&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;React&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Redact&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Saved&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;TanStack Start&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;109.3 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;62.3 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;47.0 KB (43.0%)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vite&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;68.3 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;20.1 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;48.1 KB (70.5%)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vinext&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;138.1 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;98.8 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;39.3 KB (28.5%)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Next.js&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;175.6 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;139.9 KB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;35.7 KB (20.4%)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those are the &lt;strong&gt;default&lt;/strong&gt; builds. If you turn off optional features your app doesn't use, Start drops to 59.8 KB and a client-only Vite app goes down to 12.6 KB.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The team is upfront that these are whole-build totals for a small sample app, so your percentages will vary. The benchmark source is public if you want to reproduce it. I appreciate that honesty — it's rare in launch posts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Why do 35–48 KB of gzip matter? Because that's JavaScript every user downloads, parses and executes before your app becomes interactive. On a fast laptop on fiber, sure, you won't notice. On a mid-range Android phone on a patchy mobile connection — which is a huge share of real users in markets like India — that's a noticeable chunk of your load budget, and it's coming out of the one dependency you thought you couldn't shrink.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  It's not a toy React
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My first worry with any "React but smaller" project is: what breaks? A counter demo working proves nothing. Real apps lean on hydration, Suspense, transitions, forms, error boundaries, and a pile of framework internals.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Redact supports JSX, hooks, context, refs, &lt;code&gt;memo&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;lazy&lt;/code&gt;, portals, error boundaries, streaming SSR and hydration. Pending transitions, form actions and optimistic state are covered too.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What gave me more confidence is how they test it. The compatibility suite runs real browser journeys in &lt;strong&gt;Chromium, Firefox and WebKit&lt;/strong&gt;: retained layouts, back/forward navigation, redirects, canceled navigation, Server Actions, pending forms, optimistic updates, uploads, streamed Suspense, and Fast Refresh that keeps state across edits. That's the stuff that actually breaks in production.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And you don't lose your tooling: React DevTools still works for inspection, editing and commit profiling in development.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Server Components stay with React
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is a really smart design call. With Next.js and Vinext, Redact doesn't try to replace everything. React still does server rendering and Server Components. The framework still owns routing, caching and Server Action transport. Redact picks up in the browser, hydrates the HTML, and runs your client components.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So you're not swapping out your framework's server story. You're only swapping the part that ships to your users' devices. That keeps the blast radius small, and it's exactly where the bytes matter.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Go smaller if you want to
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For Start and Vite you can tune what's included:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Start: keep hydration and router features, drop what you don't use&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;redact&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;features&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;activity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;fragmentRefs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;portal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Vite: a client-only app with no optional features&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;redact&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;preset&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;nano&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The default is a sensible "full" preset, and the docs recommend starting there and only disabling features after checking what your app (and its dependencies) actually use. Next.js and Vinext use the full runtime so framework features keep working.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  "Why not just use Preact?"
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fair question, and the Redact team answers it directly: Preact is smaller. But Redact keeps more of the modern React behavior your app may depend on, like pending transitions, deferred results and form actions. If you're on a modern React stack and you don't want to discover compatibility gaps one bug report at a time, that matters more than the last few kilobytes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The honest tradeoff
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Redact renders &lt;strong&gt;synchronously&lt;/strong&gt;. No time slicing, no interruptible renders. Suspended transitions, pending forms and optimistic state still work, but a genuinely heavy render can block input while it runs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a lot of apps, that's fine. Marketing and content sites, docs, ecommerce storefronts, typical CRUD dashboards, embedded widgets — most of their renders are small, and the bundle savings are pure win.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Where I'd be careful: screens that render huge trees in one go, or code that relies on &lt;code&gt;useTransition&lt;/code&gt; to keep typing responsive during an expensive update. The TanStack team says the same thing: try it against your busiest screens first. Their compatibility guide spells out the boundaries clearly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's not a knock on Redact. It's a clear, documented choice, and it's the reason the runtime can be this small.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why I'm a fan
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What I like most is the attitude behind it. This isn't "React is bad, switch to X." Tanner is explicit that he still believes in React and wants it to keep growing. Redact is just another runtime option inside the React ecosystem for people who want to keep their components and ship less.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's the kind of tool I want more of: respects the code you already have, gives you a real win, tells you the tradeoff upfront, and lets you walk back with one line if it doesn't fit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you have a React app on Vite, TanStack Start or Next.js, the cheapest experiment you'll run this month is:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;pnpm add @tanstack/redact&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Add &lt;code&gt;redact()&lt;/code&gt; (or &lt;code&gt;withRedact&lt;/code&gt;) to your config&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Build, compare your bundle, click through your heaviest screens&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Worst case you learn something about your app. Best case you delete tens of kilobytes from every page load without changing a single component.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Links&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Launch post: &lt;a href="https://tanstack.com/blog/introducing-tanstack-redact" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://tanstack.com/blog/introducing-tanstack-redact&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Docs: &lt;a href="https://tanstack.com/redact/latest" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://tanstack.com/redact/latest&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Compatibility guide: &lt;a href="https://tanstack.com/redact/latest/docs/compatibility" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://tanstack.com/redact/latest/docs/compatibility&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;GitHub: &lt;a href="https://github.com/TanStack/redact" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/TanStack/redact&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  TL;DR
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A React pull to refresh that holds up in a PWA needs four things: overscroll-behavior-y: contain on the scroller so Chrome does not reload the page, a touchmove listener added with { passive: false } because React's onTouchMove is passive and Safari on iOS ignores overscroll-behavior for its own refresh, a rubberband curve instead of a linear drag, and a refresh button for people who cannot drag. Pick an indicator that matches the platform (iOS spokes that move the list down, or a Material ring that floats over it) and fire one haptic tick at the threshold as an extra, never as the only signal. The Pro Wingo UI Pull To Refresh does all of this in one component.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A React pull to refresh looks like forty lines of touch handlers until it runs in a phone browser. Chrome on Android and Safari on iOS have their own pull to refresh that reloads the page, React registers &lt;code&gt;onTouchMove&lt;/code&gt; as passive so &lt;code&gt;preventDefault()&lt;/code&gt; does nothing, and a list that follows the finger one to one feels wrong on a phone. This guide fixes each one for a PWA, with the raw pattern and the Wingo UI &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/pull-to-refresh" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Pull To Refresh&lt;/a&gt; that packages it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why does a PWA need its own pull to refresh?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Because the browser's version reloads the document, and once the app is installed it may not exist at all. A reload runs every script again and drops all in-memory state: fetched data, an open sheet, the scroll position of a nested list. A data refresh only refetches the list. And a web app added to the iPhone home screen opens in standalone mode, without Safari's toolbar or its pull to refresh (our &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/blog/react-pwa-install-prompt" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;React PWA install prompt tutorial&lt;/a&gt; covers how people get it there). Discourse shipped a theme component to add one back; its &lt;a href="https://meta.discourse.org/t/ios-pwa-app-pull-to-refresh/343262" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;December 2024 announcement&lt;/a&gt; opens with "Pull to refresh is a huge gap in iOS PWA."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ready-made packages are thin. As of October 2026, a search for pull to refresh react pwa still puts &lt;a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@patlux/react-pull-to-refresh" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;@patlux/react-pull-to-refresh&lt;/a&gt; (last published November 2018) and &lt;a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/react-js-pull-to-refresh" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;react-js-pull-to-refresh&lt;/a&gt; (last published August 2022) near the top, next to React Native packages that do not run in a browser. Android calls the gesture swipe to refresh, so a React swipe to refresh component is the same thing under another name.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How do you stop the browser's own pull to refresh?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Set &lt;code&gt;overscroll-behavior-y: contain&lt;/code&gt; on the element that scrolls: &lt;code&gt;html&lt;/code&gt; when the window scrolls, the container otherwise. That one line is the whole overscroll-behavior pull to refresh fix in Chrome, which has supported the property since &lt;a href="https://developer.chrome.com/blog/overscroll-behavior" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Chrome 63&lt;/a&gt;; &lt;code&gt;contain&lt;/code&gt; keeps the overscroll glow and &lt;code&gt;none&lt;/code&gt; removes it too. Per &lt;a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/overscroll-behavior" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;MDN&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;code&gt;contain&lt;/code&gt; "disables native browser navigation, including the vertical pull-to-refresh gesture".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Safari is the exception. It has supported the property since Safari 16 (&lt;a href="https://caniuse.com/css-overscroll-behavior" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;caniuse&lt;/a&gt;), but &lt;a href="https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=275947" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;WebKit bug 275947&lt;/a&gt;, filed in June 2024 and still open when we checked in October 2026, reports that neither &lt;code&gt;none&lt;/code&gt; nor &lt;code&gt;contain&lt;/code&gt; turns off Safari's pull to refresh on iOS. What works there is JavaScript: once a touch at the top moves down, call &lt;code&gt;preventDefault()&lt;/code&gt; on its &lt;code&gt;touchmove&lt;/code&gt;, so the page does not scroll, bounce or start Safari's refresh under the finger.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is where React gets in the way. The &lt;a href="https://legacy.reactjs.org/blog/2020/10/20/react-v17.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;React 17 changelog&lt;/a&gt; lists "Keep &lt;code&gt;onTouchStart&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;onTouchMove&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;onWheel&lt;/code&gt; passive." A passive listener cannot cancel the event, so &lt;code&gt;onTouchMove={(e) =&amp;gt; e.preventDefault()}&lt;/code&gt; is silently ignored. Attach the listener yourself, on the scroller:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// React registers onTouchMove as a passive listener, so preventDefault() inside it is ignored.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// attach it yourself, on the scroller (on document, every scroll on the page would wait for this handler)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;attachPull&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;scroller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;HTMLElement&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;onPull&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;dy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;onRelease&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;startY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pulling&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;TouchEvent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pulling&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;startY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;touches&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;scroller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;scrollTop&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;touches&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;].&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;clientY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;move&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;TouchEvent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;startY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;dy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;touches&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;].&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;clientY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;startY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pulling&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;dy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// the finger went up first: a normal scroll, hand it back&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nx"&gt;startY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pulling&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;preventDefault&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// allowed because the listener is not passive&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nf"&gt;onPull&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;dy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pulling&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;onRelease&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;startY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pulling&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;previous&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;scroller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;style&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;overscrollBehaviorY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;scroller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;style&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;overscrollBehaviorY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;contain&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;scroller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;addEventListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;touchstart&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;passive&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;scroller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;addEventListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;touchmove&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;move&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;passive&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;scroller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;addEventListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;touchend&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;scroller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;addEventListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;touchcancel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;scroller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;style&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;overscrollBehaviorY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;previous&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;scroller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;removeEventListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;touchstart&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;scroller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;removeEventListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;touchmove&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;move&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;scroller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;removeEventListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;touchend&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;scroller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;removeEventListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;touchcancel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;end&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Call it from a &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; and return its cleanup. Write &lt;code&gt;dy&lt;/code&gt; to a motion value or a ref, not to state, or every &lt;code&gt;touchmove&lt;/code&gt; re-renders the list. It is a skeleton. The Wingo UI component adds what it misses: it waits 8px before deciding whether a touch is a pull, drops gestures that are more sideways than down, cancels on a second finger (a pinch is never a pull), and never starts in an input, inside &lt;code&gt;[data-no-pull]&lt;/code&gt;, in a horizontal scroller or in a nested list that is not at its own top.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How much resistance should a pull to refresh have?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Resistance that grows the further the finger goes, so the list slows down smoothly instead of following one to one into a hard stop. &lt;a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/react-simple-pull-to-refresh" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;react-simple-pull-to-refresh&lt;/a&gt;, the most recently released web package we looked at (1.3.4, January 5, 2026, MIT, React 16.10 to 19), divides the finger travel by a &lt;code&gt;resistance&lt;/code&gt; prop (default 1) and clamps it at &lt;code&gt;maxPullDownDistance&lt;/code&gt; (default 95px): a linear pull into a wall. With no dependencies, it is a fair pick for a prototype.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A native list follows a curve. The &lt;code&gt;rubberband&lt;/code&gt; helper in Wingo UI's &lt;code&gt;lib/motion&lt;/code&gt; uses an iOS-style one, &lt;code&gt;(1 - 1 / (x * 0.55 / d + 1)) * d&lt;/code&gt;, where &lt;code&gt;x&lt;/code&gt; is the finger travel past the 8px slop and &lt;code&gt;d&lt;/code&gt; the scroller height (480px at least, so a short box resists like a phone-sized one). On a 480px scroller with the defaults, 100px of finger travel moves the list 49px, 200px moves it 89px, the refresh arms at 72px (&lt;code&gt;threshold&lt;/code&gt;) after about 154px, and the list never passes &lt;code&gt;maxPull&lt;/code&gt; (128px). The pull lives in a motion value, so a &lt;code&gt;touchmove&lt;/code&gt; never re-renders your list; React state changes only when the phase does (pulling, armed, refreshing, done).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A release before the threshold springs back with the finger's velocity. A release past it parks the list at the threshold while &lt;code&gt;onRefresh&lt;/code&gt; runs (500ms at least, so the spinner never flashes), then shows a check, or an alert when the promise rejects, for &lt;code&gt;doneDuration&lt;/code&gt; (600ms) before the list glides back. Under reduced motion the list jumps to each resting point instead of springing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Arrow, iOS spokes or a Material ring: which indicator should you use?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Match the platform most of your users hold, and decide whether the content should move. The three variants of the component:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;&lt;code&gt;variant&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;The list&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;While pulling&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;While refreshing&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Good fit&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;arrow&lt;/code&gt; (default)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;moves down&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;an arrow that turns at the threshold&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;a spinner&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;neutral apps on both platforms&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;ios&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;moves down&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;12 spokes that draw one by one&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;the spokes spin in 12 steps, like UIRefreshControl&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;iPhone-first apps&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;material&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;stays put&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;a floating circle whose ring fills&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;a spinning ring in the circle&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Android-first apps, image grids, virtual lists&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;size&lt;/code&gt; sets the glyph to 20, 24 or 28px (the Material circle to 36, 40 or 44px), &lt;code&gt;tone&lt;/code&gt; colors it (neutral by default), and &lt;code&gt;showLabel&lt;/code&gt; adds a line that reads Pull to refresh, Release to refresh, Refreshing…, then Updated. Under reduced motion the spokes spin at half speed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Live demo: Pull To Refresh.&lt;/strong&gt; The original article embeds an interactive demo here (Pull the inbox down with a finger, or scroll up past the top on a trackpad: the spokes draw as you pull, the label flips to Release to refresh at the threshold, and new messages land on top). &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/blog/react-pull-to-refresh" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Try it live on wingo-ui.com&lt;/a&gt; or open the &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/pull-to-refresh" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Pull To Refresh docs&lt;/a&gt;. Install: &lt;code&gt;npx wingo-ui@latest add pull-to-refresh&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is a full inbox screen with the iOS variant around the &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/list" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;List&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;code&gt;scroll="self"&lt;/code&gt; makes the component the scroll container, with &lt;code&gt;overscroll-behavior-y: contain&lt;/code&gt; on itself. With the default &lt;code&gt;scroll="parent"&lt;/code&gt; it watches the scroller you pass or provide, else the nearest scrolling ancestor, else the window, and sets the property there while it is mounted and enabled.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

