&lt;h2&gt;


  FAQ
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  How do I disable the browser's pull to refresh in Chrome?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Set overscroll-behavior-y: contain on the element that scrolls, which is the html element when the window scrolls. Use none instead to remove the overscroll glow as well. Chrome has supported the property since version 63.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Does overscroll-behavior stop pull to refresh in Safari on iOS?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not reliably. Safari has supported the property since version 16, but WebKit bug 275947, open since June 2024, reports that it does not turn off Safari's own pull to refresh. Call preventDefault in a non-passive touchmove listener once the gesture is a pull.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Why does preventDefault not work in onTouchMove in React?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Since React 17, onTouchStart, onTouchMove and onWheel are registered as passive listeners, and browsers ignore preventDefault in a passive listener. Attach the listener yourself with addEventListener and { passive: false } in an effect, on the scrolling element.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Is a custom pull to refresh accessible?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Only with an alternative. WCAG 2.2 success criterion 2.5.7 Dragging Movements (level AA) exempts the browser's own drag to refresh but covers one you build, so add a refresh button or shortcut that runs the same reload and announce the result in a live region.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Is the Wingo UI Pull To Refresh free?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No. It is a Pro component, like the List, the Virtual List and the haptics helper it pairs with; the Spinner it uses is free. With a Pro account signed in through npx wingo-ui@latest login, npx wingo-ui@latest add pull-to-refresh copies the component and everything it imports into your project.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This article was originally published on the Wingo UI blog: &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/blog/react-pull-to-refresh" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;React Pull to Refresh in a PWA Without Fighting the Browser&lt;/a&gt;. The original has the live, interactive demos and is kept up to date.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In my last post I said my biggest regret was not starting with SSR or prerendering. This is the follow-up: what I did about it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's the problem. A React single-page app sends every visitor the same &lt;code&gt;index.html&lt;/code&gt;: a &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;div id="root"&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/div&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; and a script tag. The content only appears after JavaScript runs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Google can run JavaScript, but it does so later and less reliably. LinkedIn, WhatsApp, Slack and most other link-preview bots don't run it at all. They read the raw HTML and stop.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For ResumeOrbitz, which is a free resume builder that lives on organic search, that meant every page looked identical to a crawler. Same title, same description, almost no text.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What prerendering actually does
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Prerendering means running your app once at build time, for every route, and saving what the browser sees as a real HTML file. No server, no framework migration. You keep your SPA and get static HTML on top.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The pipeline is short:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;vite build&lt;/code&gt; produces the normal SPA in &lt;code&gt;dist/&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The script copies &lt;code&gt;dist/index.html&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;app-shell.html&lt;/code&gt;: a pure client-side shell for routes that shouldn't be prerendered (dashboard, editor).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It starts Vite's preview server on &lt;code&gt;dist/&lt;/code&gt; and launches headless Chromium with Playwright.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;For each route in &lt;code&gt;sitemap.xml&lt;/code&gt;, it loads the page, waits for React and react-helmet to finish, and grabs the full HTML.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It writes each page as a flat file: &lt;code&gt;/templates&lt;/code&gt; becomes &lt;code&gt;dist/templates.html&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Netlify serves those files directly. Protected routes fall back to &lt;code&gt;app-shell.html&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The result on ResumeOrbitz: all 105 sitemap routes now arrive with their real content, title and meta tags in the first response.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  My setup: one Playwright script, run after the build
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Everything lives in &lt;code&gt;scripts/prerender.mjs&lt;/code&gt;. npm's pre/post hooks wire it in, so a plain &lt;code&gt;npm run build&lt;/code&gt; does all three steps:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"prebuild"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"tsx scripts/generate-sitemap.ts"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"build"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"vite build"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"postbuild"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"node scripts/prerender.mjs"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The sitemap is generated first, and the prerender reads its routes straight from it, so the two can never drift apart:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getRoutesFromSitemap&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;xml&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;readFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;root&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;public/sitemap.xml&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;utf8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;matches&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;xml&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;matchAll&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;/&amp;lt;loc&amp;gt;https:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\/\/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;resumeorbitz&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;com&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;([^&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;loc&amp;gt;/g&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)];&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;matches&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The core loop, trimmed:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;for &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;route&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;of&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;routes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;goto&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;base&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;route&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;waitUntil&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;networkidle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;waitForSelector&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;#root &amp;gt; *&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;timeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;_000&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;waitForTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;800&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// let react-helmet commit its head tags&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// (the OG-tag fix goes here, see below)&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;html&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;&amp;lt;!doctype html&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\n&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;evaluate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;documentElement&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;outerHTML&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;route&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;homepageHtml&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;html&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// write the homepage last&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;writeFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;outputPathFor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;route&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;html&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;writeFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;distDir&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;index.html&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;homepageHtml&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Four lessons hide in those few lines:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A fixed wait beat a smart one.&lt;/strong&gt; I first polled until the DOM stopped changing. It sometimes "settled" in the pause before Helmet's effect even started, and saved pages without their tags. A plain 800 ms wait has been reliable.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;page.content()&lt;/code&gt; drops tags.&lt;/strong&gt; It silently left out some of Helmet's injected &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;script&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; tags, including JSON-LD schema. Reading &lt;code&gt;document.documentElement.outerHTML&lt;/code&gt; gets everything.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Write the homepage last.&lt;/strong&gt; The preview server falls back to &lt;code&gt;index.html&lt;/code&gt; for any route without a file yet. Overwrite it early, and later pages without their own SEO head inherit the homepage's canonical URL and FAQ schema.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Flat files, not folders.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;dist/templates/index.html&lt;/code&gt; makes Netlify 301-redirect &lt;code&gt;/templates&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;/templates/&lt;/code&gt;, which then disagrees with the canonical tag. &lt;code&gt;dist/templates.html&lt;/code&gt; serves at the exact sitemap URL.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The gotcha: two sets of OG tags, and the wrong one wins
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Prerendering worked. View-source showed real content. Then I looked closer at the &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;head&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; and found every page carried &lt;strong&gt;two&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;og:title&lt;/code&gt; tags: the homepage's, and its own.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The cause was in &lt;code&gt;index.html&lt;/code&gt;. Like most SPAs, mine had a static block of social tags so that &lt;em&gt;something&lt;/em&gt; showed up when people shared links:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight html"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;&amp;lt;!-- Open Graph / Social sharing --&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;meta&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;property=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"og:type"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;content=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"website"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;meta&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;property=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"og:title"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;content=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"ResumeOrbitz - Build a Resume That Gets You Hired"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;meta&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;property=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"og:description"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;content=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Create a professional, ATS-friendly resume..."&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;React-helmet doesn't replace those tags. It &lt;strong&gt;adds&lt;/strong&gt; its own per-page tags further down the &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;head&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;. So every prerendered page ended up with two &lt;code&gt;og:title&lt;/code&gt; tags: the generic one first, the correct one second.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Browsers don't care. Link-preview bots do, and many of them simply take the first tag they find. Left alone, every page on my site would have told LinkedIn it was the homepage.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The fix:&lt;/strong&gt; strip the static OG and Twitter tags during prerendering, once Helmet's per-page tags are in place. The template keeps its defaults for the non-prerendered fallback, but every prerendered page has exactly one set of tags: its own.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;evaluate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;querySelectorAll&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;meta[name="description"]:not([data-rh]), &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;meta[property^="og:"]:not([data-rh]), &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;meta[name^="twitter:"]:not([data-rh])&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;forEach&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;el&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;remove&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;());&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Helmet marks every tag it adds with &lt;code&gt;data-rh&lt;/code&gt;, so anything without it is a static leftover. &lt;code&gt;app-shell.html&lt;/code&gt; keeps its defaults because it's copied before this runs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you view-source a ResumeOrbitz page today, you can still see where the bug was. The &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;!-- Open Graph --&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;!-- Twitter Card --&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; comments are there, with nothing underneath, and the real tags sit lower down, marked &lt;code&gt;data-rh="true"&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Check your own site in 2 minutes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You don't need any tools beyond a terminal:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;# 1. Does the raw HTML have real content, or an empty root?&lt;/span&gt;
curl &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-s&lt;/span&gt; https://yoursite.com/some-page | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;grep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-o&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'&amp;lt;h1[^&amp;gt;]*&amp;gt;[^&amp;lt;]*'&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c"&gt;# 2. How many og:title tags does the page ship? It should be exactly 1.&lt;/span&gt;
curl &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-s&lt;/span&gt; https://yoursite.com/some-page | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;grep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'property="og:title"'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Then paste a URL into &lt;a href="https://www.linkedin.com/post-inspector/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;LinkedIn's Post Inspector&lt;/a&gt;. It shows exactly what LinkedIn's bot reads, and refreshes its cached preview while it's at it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If check 2 returns &lt;code&gt;2&lt;/code&gt;, you have the same bug I had.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I'd tell past me
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you're starting a content-heavy React app today, pick a framework with SSR or static generation from day one. If you're already deep into a Vite SPA like I was, a prerender script is a good middle path: one file, no rewrite.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Just check your &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;head&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; after you do it. The page can look perfect in the browser while every share bot reads the wrong tags.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Disclosure: I'm the founder of &lt;a href="https://resumeorbitz.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ResumeOrbitz&lt;/a&gt;, the site used as the example here.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Over to you: did you go SSR or prerender for your React app? And did the OG tags bite you too?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;JavaScript is one of the most important programming languages used in modern &lt;a href="https://dotsimplify.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;web development&lt;/a&gt;. It plays a crucial role in making websites interactive, dynamic, and user-friendly. While HTML provides the structure of a webpage and CSS handles its design and appearance, JavaScript adds functionality and enables users to interact with different website elements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fy6ki2aab4ivbru3ggz0y.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fy6ki2aab4ivbru3ggz0y.jpg" alt=" " width="799" height="454"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a href="https://dev.tourl"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
From displaying interactive menus and animations to developing complex web applications, JavaScript has become an essential technology for creating modern digital experiences.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Making Websites Interactive&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One of the primary roles of JavaScript is to make websites interactive. It allows developers to create elements that respond to user actions, such as clicking buttons, submitting forms, scrolling pages, and selecting options.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Displaying pop-up messages.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Creating dropdown menus.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Validating forms before submission.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Showing and hiding website content.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These features improve user engagement and make websites easier to navigate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Dynamic Content Updates&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;JavaScript allows developers to update website content without reloading the entire webpage. Using the Document Object Model (DOM), developers can modify HTML elements, change text, update images, and adjust styles dynamically.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, an e-commerce website can instantly update the shopping cart quantity when a customer adds a product.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Form Validation&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;JavaScript helps validate user input before sending information to the server. It can check email formats, passwords, phone numbers, and mandatory fields.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This reduces unnecessary server requests and helps users correct errors immediately. However, server-side validation is also necessary for security and data accuracy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Asynchronous Communication&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;JavaScript supports asynchronous communication through technologies such as Fetch API and AJAX. These technologies allow websites to exchange data with servers without refreshing the entire page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Loading new posts on social media.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Displaying live search suggestions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Updating weather information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fetching product details from an online store.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This makes websites faster and provides a smoother browsing experience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Developing Modern Web Applications&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;JavaScript is widely used to develop complex web applications. Popular frameworks and libraries such as React.js, Angular, and Vue.js help developers build scalable, reusable, and efficient user interfaces.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These technologies are commonly used in:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;E-commerce platforms.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Social networking websites.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Online banking applications.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Content management systems.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Business dashboards.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Server-Side Development&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;JavaScript is not limited to frontend development. With Node.js, developers can also use JavaScript on the server side.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It helps build APIs, handle database operations, manage authentication, and process application requests. This allows developers to use a single programming language across both frontend and backend development.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Improving User Experience&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://dotsimplify.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;JavaScript&lt;/a&gt; enables features such as animations, interactive maps, sliders, real-time notifications, and multimedia controls. These functionalities make websites more engaging and improve the overall user experience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AI is weirdly confident about React hooks. Ask for “extract the data fetching into a custom hook” and you’ll get something that &lt;em&gt;looks&lt;/em&gt; idiomatic: &lt;code&gt;useSomething&lt;/code&gt;, a &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;, a couple of &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt;s, maybe a &lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt; for good measure. Types pass. The demo works once. Then you merge and discover the effect fires twice on every keystroke, or the cleanup never ran, or the hook is just a wrapper around three lines that didn’t need a name.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I treat AI-suggested hooks like AI-suggested refactors: useful drafts, not mergeable by default. Here’s the smell checklist I run before I accept the branch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Smell 1: &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; dependency lies
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The model loves this pattern:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;fetchUser&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;userId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;then&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;setUser&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[]);&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// "run once on mount"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Except &lt;code&gt;userId&lt;/code&gt; changed three routes ago, and the effect still holds the first id. Or the opposite lie:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;analytics&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;track&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;view&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// object identity thrash&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Every parent re-render invents a new &lt;code&gt;item&lt;/code&gt; object, so “track once” becomes “track forever.”&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What I check:&lt;/strong&gt; for every &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;, I ask “what should actually retrigger this?” If the answer is “once,” I want an explicit id/primitive in the deps, or an abortable fetch keyed by that id — not an empty array with a closed-over value. If the answer is “when X changes,” I want &lt;code&gt;X&lt;/code&gt; to be a stable primitive or a memoized value I trust, not a fresh object from JSX.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Smell 2: unstable callbacks as dependencies
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Classic AI move: wrap a handler in &lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt;, then put that callback in another hook’s deps… while the callback itself depends on props that change every render.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;onSave&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useCallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;save&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;formState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;formState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// formState changes constantly&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nb"&gt;window&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;addEventListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;keydown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;onSave&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;window&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;removeEventListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;keydown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;onSave&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;onSave&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// rebinds every keystroke of formState&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt; didn’t stabilize anything. It just moved the thrash one layer down.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What I check:&lt;/strong&gt; if a callback is only a dep because another effect needs it, I prefer reading the latest value from a ref, or narrowing the callback’s deps to the primitives it actually needs. “Add &lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt;” is not a fix by itself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Smell 3: derived state stuffed into &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The agent sees &lt;code&gt;fullName&lt;/code&gt; used in three places and invents:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fullName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setFullName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setFullName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;first&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;last&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;first&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;last&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That’s derived data. It doesn’t need state. It needs:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fullName&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;first&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;last&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Or a &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt; if the derivation is actually expensive (it almost never is for string concat).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What I check:&lt;/strong&gt; any &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt; + &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; pair that only syncs from other props/state is guilty until proven otherwise. AI does this a lot because “state” feels like the React vocabulary for “a value I can name.”&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Smell 4: missing cleanup (or fake cleanup)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fetch-in-effect without abort. Subscriptions without unsubscribe. &lt;code&gt;setTimeout&lt;/code&gt; without clear. The demo looks fine because you never navigate away mid-request.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;cancelled&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;load&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;then&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;cancelled&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setData&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;cancelled&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That’s the minimum. Better: &lt;code&gt;AbortController&lt;/code&gt; so the network work actually stops. AI often ships the happy path only.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What I check:&lt;/strong&gt; every effect that starts async work, timers, or listeners must have a cleanup that matches. I also watch for “cleanup” that only sets a flag but still lets expensive work continue — fine for setState races, not fine for upload loops.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Smell 5: over-abstraction into &lt;code&gt;useX&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not every three lines deserve a hook.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useIsOpen&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;initial&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;open&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setOpen&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;initial&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;open&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;open&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setOpen&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;close&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setOpen&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Cute. Also a digression in code review. Custom hooks earn their name when they encapsulate a &lt;strong&gt;non-obvious contract&lt;/strong&gt;: syncing with a store, coordinating multiple effects, sharing behavior across distant components. “Boolean with open/close” is just state.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What I check:&lt;/strong&gt; would I explain this hook in a PR description without sounding silly? If the hook exists only so the component file is shorter, I inline it. AI loves extracting; I love deleting.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Smell 6: stale closures dressed as “helpers”
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useSearch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[])&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setQuery&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;includes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setQuery&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;results&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Looks fine until &lt;code&gt;filter&lt;/code&gt; gets passed to a child as a callback and the child memoizes it, or until someone moves &lt;code&gt;filter&lt;/code&gt; into a &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;[]&lt;/code&gt;. Closures over &lt;code&gt;items&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;query&lt;/code&gt; go stale in ways that are hard to see in a generated diff.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What I check:&lt;/strong&gt; values computed from props/state should either be plain expressions in render, or memoized with the &lt;em&gt;right&lt;/em&gt; deps — not casually closed over inside nested functions the agent plans to “reuse.”&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Smell 7: “just add another &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt;”
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When something re-renders too often, the model’s first instinct is memoization:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;sorted&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useMemo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;].&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sort&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;byName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;items&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;visible&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useMemo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;sorted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;isVisible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;sorted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;isVisible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useMemo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;visible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;toRow&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;visible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Three memos, still thrashing if &lt;code&gt;items&lt;/code&gt; is a new array every parent render. Memo is a tax you pay after you’ve fixed identity and state shape — not a perfume you spray on the smell.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What I check:&lt;/strong&gt; before accepting a memo, I ask what identity is unstable upstream. Fix that first. Keep &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt; for real work (big lists, expensive pure transforms), not for &lt;code&gt;obj.a + obj.b&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Smell 8: hooks that hide side effects
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The scariest AI hooks look pure in the name and impure in the body:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useCartTotal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;cart&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Cart&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;localStorage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setItem&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;cart-total&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;String&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;cart&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;total&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;cart&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;total&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;cart&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;lines&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;reduce&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;l&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;l&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;price&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Callers think they’re reading a number. They’re also writing storage. Side effects buried in “getter” hooks are how you get “why did navigating to settings clear my draft?” bugs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What I check:&lt;/strong&gt; naming. &lt;code&gt;useX&lt;/code&gt; that returns data should not silently write, fetch, or mutate globals. If it does I/O, the name should say so (&lt;code&gt;usePersistCartTotal&lt;/code&gt;) and the call site should look intentional.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  My merge gate (short version)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before I merge an AI-touched hook, I want yes on all of these:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Deps match the real trigger (no empty-array lies, no object thrash)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt; for values that are pure derivations&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Async / listeners / timers have matching cleanup&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The hook earns its name (shared or non-obvious contract)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No drive-by &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt; pile-on&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Side effects are obvious from the name and the call site&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If two or more fail, I don’t “tweak the hook.” I ask the agent for a smaller change: inline the logic, or rewrite the effect with an explicit contract in the prompt.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Prompt that produces fewer smelly hooks
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When I &lt;em&gt;do&lt;/em&gt; want a custom hook from the model, I fence it:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Extract a hook only if the behavior is reused or the effect graph is non-trivial.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Prefer derived values over syncing state in effects.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Every &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; must list real deps and a cleanup.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Do not add &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt;/&lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt; unless you can point to a measured or structural reason.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
No hidden I/O in hooks named like getters.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That doesn’t make the model perfect. It makes the first draft close enough that the smell checklist is a quick pass instead of a rewrite.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hooks are easy to generate and expensive to debug after merge. Five minutes of smell-checking beats an afternoon of “why does this fire twice in Strict Mode only on the settings page.”&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Un estado de carga parece una pieza pequeña de la interfaz. Sin embargo, decide si una persona entiende lo que ocurre o pulsa el botón varias veces. En un formulario de registro, por ejemplo, la espera puede incluir una petición, una validación y la llegada de un email. Si cada paso cambia el layout, el producto se siente lento aunque la red no sea el principal problema.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Mi regla práctica es esta: una carga debe conservar el espacio, explicar el siguiente paso y tener una salida clara si algo falla. No hace falta una animación espectacular. Hace falta que el diseño, React y la accesibilidad cuenten la misma historia.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  El problema no es esperar, es no entender la espera
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Un patrón bastante común muestra un spinner después de pulsar “Continuar”. El botón desaparece, aparece un texto debajo y, unos milisegundos después, todo vuelve a su sitio. Ese salto visual aumenta la sensación de inestabilidad y puede hacer que alguien pulse otra vez.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;También hay otro fallo: usar el mismo estado para “estamos comprobando”, “se envió el email” y “no pudimos completar la acción”. Son situaciones diferentes. El usuario necesita saber si debe esperar, revisar su bandeja o corregir un dato.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;En flujos de QA aparecen búsquedas como &lt;code&gt;tempmailso&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;temp mail so&lt;/code&gt; y hasta el typo &lt;code&gt;tempail&lt;/code&gt;. No son estados de la interfaz, claro, pero sirven para recordar que la parte de email tiene varios pasos. El frontend no debería presentar todos como un simple “cargando”.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Un estado de carga que conserva el layout
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Primero defino un contrato pequeño para el componente:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;idle&lt;/code&gt;: el formulario acepta interacción.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;loading&lt;/code&gt;: la petición está en curso y la acción se puede bloquear.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;success&lt;/code&gt;: la operación terminó y se explica qué hacer ahora.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;error&lt;/code&gt;: hay un mensaje útil y una acción para intentarlo de nuevo.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;El espacio reservado es importante. En vez de insertar un aviso que empuja el formulario, dejo un contenedor con una altura mínima. Así, el botón y los campos no bailan cuando cambia el mensaje. Es una decisión de CSS, pero mejora el rendimiento percibido sin fingir que la operación terminó antes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Un componente sencillo puede ser suficiente:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;idle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;loading&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;success&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;StatusMessageProps&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;StatusMessage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;StatusMessageProps&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;idle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"status-space"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;aria-hidden&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"true"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`status status--&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;role&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;alert&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;aria-live&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;assertive&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;polite&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;El estado &lt;code&gt;idle&lt;/code&gt; conserva el hueco, mientras que los demás estados ofrecen información. En una aplicación real, evitaría que &lt;code&gt;message&lt;/code&gt; sea una cadena genérica como “Error”. El texto debería decir qué pasó y cuál es la siguiente acción, por ejemplo: “No recibimos respuesta. Intenta de nuevo en unos segundos”.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  React y CSS para feedback accesible
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;El atributo &lt;code&gt;aria-busy&lt;/code&gt; puede indicar que una región está siendo actualizada. No lo pondría en toda la página: prefiero aplicarlo al bloque que realmente cambia.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;section&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;aria-busy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;loading&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;aria-labelledby&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"signup-title"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;h2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"signup-title"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;Crear cuenta&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;h2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;EmailForm&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;disabled&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;loading&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;StatusMessage&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;section&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Para el estilo, una reserva mínima y una animación discreta suelen bastar:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight css"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;.status-space&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="py"&gt;min-block-size&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;2.75rem&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nc"&gt;.status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="py"&gt;min-block-size&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;2.75rem&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="py"&gt;margin-block&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;0.75rem&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;padding&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;0.65rem&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;0.8rem&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;border-radius&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;0.4rem&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nc"&gt;.status--loading&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;color&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;#394150&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;background&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;#f1f4f8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;@media&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;prefers-reduced-motion&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;reduce&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nc"&gt;.status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;animation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;none&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Hay un detalle que se olvida facil: el contraste y el foco. Un color amarillo claro puede verse “amable” en el diseño, pero no comunica nada si el texto se pierde. También conviene revisar el flujo con teclado y con un lector de pantalla; el spinner por si solo no explica el progreso.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Cuando el proceso depende de varios eventos, una evidencia separada ayuda al equipo. Por ejemplo, puedes guardar un &lt;a href="https://dev.to/silviutech/agentes-llm-un-recibo-para-emails-de-prueba-3l5i"&gt;recibo de email para pruebas automatizadas&lt;/a&gt; y conectar ese resultado con el estado visible. Si el backend necesita aislamiento entre entornos, revisa también el patrón de &lt;a href="https://dev.to/oliviachen7/fastapi-emails-aislados-por-branch-3i51-temp-slug-9747599?preview=15d0f24ab23156ff7add433341db5b88816d63c95c3ecff5b0582256604f6e1410fd3181238aaa64757da0be78c0c8b8b797801df629e31b80e4e55a"&gt;emails aislados por rama de desarrollo&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Medir la experiencia sin inventar precisión
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No mediría solo el tiempo hasta que desaparece el spinner. Una vista más util es registrar:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;cuánto tarda en aparecer el primer feedback;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;cuánto tiempo permanece cada estado;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;cuántas veces se repite la acción;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;cuántos errores terminan en reintento o abandono.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Estas señales ayudan a separar un problema de backend de uno de comunicación. Si la respuesta llega rapido pero muchas personas repiten el clic, quizá el botón no muestra suficiente confirmación. Si el estado de error aparece sin una acción clara, el tiempo de red no cuenta toda la historia.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Checklist antes de publicar
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿El layout conserva el espacio durante la carga?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿El botón tiene foco y queda deshabilitado solo cuando corresponde?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿El mensaje diferencia espera, éxito y error?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿Un lector de pantalla recibe el cambio sin demasiados anuncios?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿La interfaz funciona con teclado y con &lt;code&gt;prefers-reduced-motion&lt;/code&gt;?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;¿El test cubre una respuesta lenta y un reintento?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Q&amp;amp;A rápido
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  ¿Siempre necesito un skeleton?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No. Un skeleton funciona cuando conoces la forma del contenido que llegará. Para una acción breve, un mensaje estable y un botón con estado claro suele ser más honesto.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  ¿Debo mostrar porcentaje de progreso?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Solo si puedes calcularlo de forma fiable. Un porcentaje inventado crea más dudas. Para una petición de email, “Comprobando el envío” puede ser mejor que avanzar de 10% a 90% sin evidencia.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  ¿Qué aporta CSS al rendimiento?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No reduce por sí solo el tiempo del servidor, pero puede evitar saltos, repintados innecesarios y confusión. Esa combinación hace que la interfaz parezca más rapida y, sobre todo, sea más entendible.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AI code generators write broken accessibility by default, but not because LLMs are incapable of writing semantic HTML. The issue is that an LLM does not know when it has produced inaccessible code, and most developers do not verify what it outputs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built 14 production Next.js applications with Claude Code over the last six months. Every single one passes WCAG 2.1 Level AA across roughly 350 unique screens, using Next.js 16, Tailwind CSS 4, and shadcn/ui. Every app passes axe-core with zero critical or serious violations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This worked because I set up a verification workflow that catches Claude's mistakes the same way you would catch a junior engineer's mistakes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The myth about AI and accessibility
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The common claim on developer forums is that AI code generators cannot produce accessible code because LLMs hallucinate ARIA attributes and ignore semantic HTML.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is half true. LLMs do hallucinate ARIA attributes. Claude has generated &lt;code&gt;role="container"&lt;/code&gt; (which does not exist) and &lt;code&gt;aria-pressed="yes"&lt;/code&gt; (which should be &lt;code&gt;"true"&lt;/code&gt;) right in front of me. I have watched it output buttons as divs, form inputs without labels, and contrast ratios failing at 2.1:1.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The flaw in the argument is the conclusion. AI generates broken code, but so do humans. Experienced developers catch their own mistakes because they review their work against a checklist and run automated tooling. Most teams using AI skip both.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The solution is not to stop using AI. It is to enforce a repeatable review discipline.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 1: Start with an accessibility-first system prompt
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Claude Code follows the priorities defined in your system prompt. If you omit accessibility requirements, the model optimizes for visual layout and generation speed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I add this block to &lt;code&gt;CLAUDE.md&lt;/code&gt; in every project before asking Claude to write code:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight markdown"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="gh"&gt;# Accessibility requirements (NON-NEGOTIABLE)&lt;/span&gt;

Every component you write must pass WCAG 2.1 AA. Specifically:
&lt;span class="p"&gt;
1.&lt;/span&gt; Use semantic HTML before reaching for ARIA. &lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;button&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; not &lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;onclick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;.
   &lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;nav&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; not &lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;role=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"navigation"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;. &lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;main&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; not &lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"main"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;.
&lt;span class="p"&gt;
2.&lt;/span&gt; Every interactive element must work with keyboard alone. Tab to focus,
   Enter/Space to activate, Esc to close. Test by mentally walking through
   the tab order.
&lt;span class="p"&gt;
3.&lt;/span&gt; Every form input must have an associated &lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;label&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;. Placeholders are
   not labels.
&lt;span class="p"&gt;
4.&lt;/span&gt; Color contrast: 4.5:1 for normal text, 3:1 for large text and UI
   components. Use the design tokens from globals.css; do not invent
   new colors.
&lt;span class="p"&gt;
5.&lt;/span&gt; Focus indicators must be visible against every background the
   component can sit on. Ring-2 with offset, never ring-1 with opacity.
&lt;span class="p"&gt;
6.&lt;/span&gt; Status changes must be announced to screen readers via aria-live
   or role="status". Toasts use sonner with role="status".
&lt;span class="p"&gt;
7.&lt;/span&gt; Never use color alone to convey meaning. Always pair color with
   icon or text.
&lt;span class="p"&gt;
8.&lt;/span&gt; Modals trap focus, return focus to trigger on close, close on Esc.
   Use shadcn Dialog (built on Radix), not custom implementations.
&lt;span class="p"&gt;
9.&lt;/span&gt; Touch targets are at least 44x44px on mobile. Use min-h-11 on tap
   targets that look smaller.
&lt;span class="p"&gt;
10.&lt;/span&gt; Test the following before marking anything complete:
&lt;span class="p"&gt;    -&lt;/span&gt; Tab through every interactive element
&lt;span class="p"&gt;    -&lt;/span&gt; Resize browser to 320px wide (no horizontal scroll)
&lt;span class="p"&gt;    -&lt;/span&gt; Toggle dark mode (contrast still passes)
&lt;span class="p"&gt;    -&lt;/span&gt; Run the page through axe DevTools (zero criticals)
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Claude still makes mistakes with this in place, but they happen far less often, and the model fixes them faster when reminded of its own rules.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 2: Use shadcn/ui as the base instead of custom components
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;shadcn/ui wraps Radix UI primitives. Radix handles focus management, keyboard navigation, ARIA attributes, and screen reader announcements out of the box.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Whenever a screen needs a button, modal, dropdown, dialog, or popover, use the shadcn implementation. Never let Claude build a custom modal or dropdown from scratch. It will eventually drop the focus trap or miss the &lt;code&gt;aria-expanded&lt;/code&gt; attribute.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 3: Run axe-core on every screen
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After Claude generates a screen, run axe DevTools on the rendered page. It takes a few minutes and catches roughly 60% of automated accessibility issues.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To automate this in your test suite, use &lt;code&gt;@axe-core/playwright&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;test&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;expect&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@playwright/test&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;AxeBuilder&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@axe-core/playwright&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;test&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;dashboard has no a11y violations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;goto&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;/dashboard&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;results&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;AxeBuilder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;withTags&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;wcag2a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;wcag2aa&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;wcag21a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;wcag21aa&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;wcag22aa&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;])&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;analyze&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;expect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;results&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;violations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toEqual&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([])&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;When this test fails, copy the exact violation details back into Claude Code and tell it to fix them. Claude is bad at preventing violations on the initial generation, but it is reliable at fixing them when given the exact failing rule.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 4: Run a manual keyboard test
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Static tools miss dynamic interaction bugs. A screen can pass axe-core while remaining unusable without a mouse. For every screen:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Navigate to the page.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Press Tab repeatedly through every interactive element.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Check that the tab order follows the visual reading order.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Trigger actions using only the keyboard: Enter or Space for buttons, arrow keys for menus, Esc to dismiss overlays.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Watch for elements that trap focus inappropriately or drop focus entirely.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This takes about 90 seconds per view and catches broken dialog traps or menus that fail to focus their first item on open.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 5: Run a screen reader smoke test
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Turn on VoiceOver on macOS (Cmd+F5) or NVDA on Windows and walk the main user flow. Check three things:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Meaningful labels: every interactive item announces a clear label, not just "button" or "edit text".&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Dynamic updates: loading spinners, form error states, and success toasts trigger announcements.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Headings: the hierarchy is logical, starting with one &lt;code&gt;h1&lt;/code&gt;, followed by &lt;code&gt;h2&lt;/code&gt; sections, without skipped levels.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 6: Test color contrast across all themes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;shadcn relies on CSS custom properties for theming. A color pair that passes contrast in light mode can easily fail in dark mode.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The most frequent regression is &lt;code&gt;muted-foreground&lt;/code&gt; placed on top of &lt;code&gt;muted&lt;/code&gt; backgrounds. Check every theme permutation explicitly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Common Claude mistakes and the fix prompts
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Across hundreds of generated screens, Claude repeatedly falls into the same specific traps:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  1. Clickable divs
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Claude will build a card as a &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;div&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; with an &lt;code&gt;onClick&lt;/code&gt; handler. It looks right visually, but screen readers and keyboards ignore it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Prompt: "Make every clickable card a &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;button&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; or wrap it in a &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;Link&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; from next/link, never a div with onClick."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  2. Skipped heading levels
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Claude often puts an &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;h1&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; on the title and jumps immediately to &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;h3&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; for cards to match visual styling, violating WCAG 1.3.1.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Prompt: "Heading levels must be sequential. After h1, the next level can only be h2. After h2, only h2 or h3. Never skip a level."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  3. Placeholders instead of labels
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Inputs will get &lt;code&gt;placeholder="Email address"&lt;/code&gt; with no &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;label&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; element. Once the user types, the placeholder disappears, and screen readers lose context.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Prompt: "Every form input must have a &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;label&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; element associated via &lt;code&gt;htmlFor&lt;/code&gt;. Use the shadcn &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;Label&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; component. The placeholder is for example text only, never as the label."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  4. Custom modals without focus traps
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If Claude writes a modal without Radix primitives, tabbing escapes the overlay into background elements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Prompt: "Always use the shadcn &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;Dialog&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; component for modals. Never build a custom modal. Radix Dialog handles focus trap, focus return, and Esc-to-close correctly."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  5. Silent asynchronous updates
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Claude generates a "Saving..." text label on submit, but omits an announcement region.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Prompt: "Status messages must be in a region with &lt;code&gt;role='status'&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;aria-live='polite'&lt;/code&gt; so screen readers announce them. For toast notifications, use sonner which handles this automatically."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  6. Relying purely on color
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Inputs fail with a red border alone, without an error message or icon, which violates WCAG 1.4.1.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Prompt: "Status indicators must combine color, icon, and text. Failed inputs need a red border AND an error message AND an aria-invalid attribute. Successful submits need a green icon AND text AND a status announcement."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  7. Hallucinated ARIA
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Claude writes attributes that do not exist in the WAI-ARIA specification, such as &lt;code&gt;aria-tooltip&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;aria-hidden="yes"&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Prompt: "Pay especially close attention to attribute names and boolean values. Ensure every ARIA attribute matches the W3C specification exactly."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What cannot be delegated to Claude
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Claude Code handled the bulk of the initial markup, but specific tasks required manual implementation:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Recharts SVGs: Chart graphics have no accessible fallback out of the box. You must write visually hidden &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;table&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; summaries alongside each chart so screen readers can consume the underlying dataset.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Dynamic announcement copy: Specific announcements like "Filter applied, showing 47 results" get missed by LLMs unless explicitly requested.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Physical screen reader listening: No automated tool replaces testing an interface with VoiceOver or NVDA to verify the spoken reading order.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This is a shorter version of a guide I wrote at Frontkit Studio. The &lt;a href="https://thefrontkit.com/blogs/how-i-built-14-accessible-nextjs-apps-with-claude-code?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=syndication" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;full post&lt;/a&gt; has the complete walkthrough.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
